Subject: [PATCH] execve: the brk heap went back under a live signal frame

The exec reclaim gives the guest's heap back to where it started, so
that an exec chain does not accumulate one heap per program. The frame
the kernel built for the trap is the guest's, and the guest returns
into the new program through it: the SIGSYS tier answers the trapped
execve by rewriting the frame the kernel delivered, and the guest's
rt_sigreturn then reads it back.

The kernel puts that frame on the guest's alternate signal stack
whenever the guest registered one and the handler asked for it, and
where that stack is comes from the guest's own allocator. On an Android
guest that is the heap the reclaim is about to shrink. Giving the heap
back unmapped the frame, and the write of the new program's registers
into it faulted: SIGSEGV on the first exec, measured.

The sweep already knew this. cng_scr_hit keeps a mapping that holds a
live frame out of the sweep, which is why the frame survived it. The brk
shrink asked no such question, and it is the one that unmapped the
frame: the heap is a mapping of its own, and brk(cng_g_brk0) unmaps
everything above the initial break whatever the sweep thought of it.

SS_ONSTACK does not answer the question it looks like it answers. It
says the interrupted stack pointer was inside the alternate stack,
which holds only when the trap was taken while the thread already ran
on it. A trap taken on the thread's ordinary stack is delivered *to*
the alternate stack and comes back with SS_ONSTACK clear -- the common
case, and the one that faulted. The kernel writes the frame's uc_stack
from the thread's own registration (unsafe_save_altstack), and the
registration says nothing about where the frame went: ss_sp and
ss_size name the alternate stack, and the frame is in it.

So the test is geometric. cng_scr_frame_ceiling asks whether the frame
is inside the alternate stack the ucontext names, and keeps that stack
whole when it is. The whole stack, not just the bytes of the ucontext:
the frame is a siginfo and a ucontext side by side, and the siginfo
sits below the ucontext (struct rt_sigframe is { siginfo info;
ucontext uc; }), so an extent that began at the ucontext would leave
it out. Where no alternate stack holds the frame, the ucontext's own
extent is kept.

cng_exec_reset now shrinks the heap to the ceiling of every live frame
rather than to the initial break, and leaves the heap where it is
where the ceiling reaches it. The heap is still given back: everything
below the frame is reclaimed, and a guest with no live frame at the
reclaim gets the whole heap back as before.

Compiled for aarch64 with -Werror. The suite needs aarch64 and this
host has neither qemu-user nor an aarch64 device, so the change was
not run against it here; the arithmetic was checked against the
addresses in the core that caught it, and the reclaim keeps the frame
it used to unmap.
---
 include/cng/monitor.h |  4 +++
 src/monitor/execve.c  | 18 +++++++++++--
 src/monitor/sigsys.c  | 59 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 79 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/cng/monitor.h b/include/cng/monitor.h
index c577d05..494ad82 100644
--- a/include/cng/monitor.h
+++ b/include/cng/monitor.h
@@ -627,6 +627,10 @@ int cng_scratch_slot_for(long tid, unsigned long *hi_out);
 /* Does [lo, hi) touch a scratch stack? Asked by the exec reclaim, which
  * must not give a slot back as if it were the outgoing program's. */
 int cng_scr_hit(unsigned long lo, unsigned long hi);
+/* The top of every live signal frame below `limit`, or 0 when there is none:
+ * the exec reclaim must not give the heap back under a frame the guest still
+ * returns through. */
+unsigned long cng_scr_frame_ceiling(unsigned long limit);
 
 /* Install the SIGSYS handler alone (no filter, no probes): testing. */
 int cng_sigsys_install_only(void);
diff --git a/src/monitor/execve.c b/src/monitor/execve.c
index f79456b..36d06c7 100644
--- a/src/monitor/execve.c
+++ b/src/monitor/execve.c
@@ -1505,8 +1505,22 @@ static void cng_exec_reset(void) {
                 24 /* sizeof(struct robust_list_head) */, 0, 0, 0, 0);
     if (cng_g_brk0) {
         long cur = CNG_SYS(__NR_brk, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0);
-        if (cur > 0 && (unsigned long)cur > cng_g_brk0)
-            CNG_SYS(__NR_brk, cng_g_brk0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0);
+        if (cur > 0 && (unsigned long)cur > cng_g_brk0) {
+            /* The heap goes back to where it started, but not under a signal
+             * frame the guest still has to return through. The frame the kernel
+             * built for this trap lies on the guest's alternate signal stack,
+             * and the guest's own allocator may have put that stack in the
+             * heap. Shrinking past it unmapped the frame, and the write of the
+             * new program's registers into it faulted: SIGSEGV on the first
+             * exec, measured. The ceiling is the top of every live frame; where
+             * it reaches, the heap stays where it is. */
+            unsigned long target = cng_g_brk0;
+            unsigned long ceiling = cng_scr_frame_ceiling((unsigned long)cur);
+            if (ceiling > target)
+                target = cng_page_up(ceiling);
+            if ((unsigned long)cur > target)
+                CNG_SYS(__NR_brk, target, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0);
+        }
     }
 }
 
diff --git a/src/monitor/sigsys.c b/src/monitor/sigsys.c
index 6adfdc5..5455542 100644
--- a/src/monitor/sigsys.c
+++ b/src/monitor/sigsys.c
@@ -773,6 +773,65 @@ int cng_scr_hit(unsigned long lo, unsigned long hi) {
     return 0;
 }
 
+/* The top of every live signal frame below `limit`.
+ *
+ * The frame the kernel built for a trap belongs to the guest: the SIGSYS tier
+ * returns into the new program through rt_sigreturn, and what that reads is
+ * the frame the kernel built when it delivered the trap. The kernel puts that
+ * frame on the guest's alternate signal stack whenever the guest registered one
+ * and the handler asked for it — and where that stack is comes from the guest's
+ * own allocator, which on an Android guest is the heap. Giving the heap back
+ * unmapped the frame, and the write of the new program's registers into it
+ * faulted: SIGSEGV on the first exec, measured.
+ *
+ * SS_ONSTACK does not answer this. It says the interrupted stack pointer was
+ * inside the alternate stack, which holds only when the trap was taken while
+ * the thread already ran on it. A trap taken on the thread's ordinary stack is
+ * delivered *to* the alternate stack and comes back with SS_ONSTACK clear —
+ * the common case, and the one that faulted: the kernel writes the frame's
+ * uc_stack from the thread's own registration (unsafe_save_altstack), and the
+ * registration says nothing about where the frame went. The test is therefore
+ * geometric: is the frame inside the alternate stack the ucontext names?
+ *
+ * The whole alternate stack is kept, not just the bytes of the ucontext: the
+ * frame is a siginfo and a ucontext side by side, and the siginfo sits below
+ * the ucontext (struct rt_sigframe is { siginfo info; ucontext uc; }), so an
+ * extent that began at the ucontext would leave it out. Keeping the stack costs
+ * one alternate stack and not a chain of them: the frame is dead one generation
+ * later, when the next trap's frame is somewhere else.
+ *
+ * `limit` is the top of the region being given back. A frame above it is not
+ * in that region, and the reclaim cannot reach it. */
+unsigned long cng_scr_frame_ceiling(unsigned long limit) {
+    unsigned long ceiling = 0;
+    for (int i = 0; i < CNG_SCR_N; i++) {
+        struct cng_ucontext *uc = (struct cng_ucontext *)
+            __atomic_load_n(&cng_scr[i].uc, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
+        if (!uc)
+            continue; /* no frame on this slot */
+        unsigned long u = (unsigned long)uc;
+        if (u >= limit)
+            continue; /* above the region: the reclaim cannot reach it */
+        unsigned long top = u + sizeof(struct cng_ucontext);
+        unsigned long sp = (unsigned long)uc->uc_stack.ss_sp;
+        unsigned long size = uc->uc_stack.ss_size;
+        /* u - sp < size, rather than u < sp + size, so that a stack whose end
+         * wraps cannot claim a frame it does not hold. */
+        if (size && sp && !(uc->uc_stack.ss_flags & CNG_SS_DISABLE) &&
+            u >= sp && u - sp < size) {
+            /* The frame is on the alternate stack: keep all of it. */
+            unsigned long end = sp + size;
+            if (end > top)
+                top = end;
+        }
+        if (top > limit)
+            top = limit;
+        if (top > ceiling)
+            ceiling = top;
+    }
+    return ceiling;
+}
+
 /* Is `tid` still a thread of this process? tgkill with signal 0 does the
  * existence check and delivers nothing — one syscall, where a /proc read would
  * be several, and this runs inside the handler. */
-- 
2.56.0

